{"description":"The authoritative standard corpus contradicts itself on its own version. `specification/overview.md` states \"This specification is the versioned snapshot of the EKA Standard 1.1\" and later `### Version` / `1.1.`; `specification/exchange.md` states \"Standard corpus version: 1.1 (this specification)\". Meanwhile `EKAv2` declares `Version 1.3`, `specification/versioning.md` states the snapshot is 1.3, and `CHANGELOG.md` documents 1.3 as current. Because `specification/` is declared authoritative on any conflict, the winner of every conflict disagrees with itself.","impact":"A reader instructed to trust the specification above all else cannot resolve which version is normative, and a third-party implementer may target 1.1 semantics. The drift is four stale references, so the fix is mechanical: correct overview.md and exchange.md to 1.3. The mechanism preventing recurrence (a generated version manifest with a test) is deliberately deferred to wave 2 so this stays a narrow prose correction."}